  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "Given the linear transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\u27f6[\\begin{aligned}-3x-6y \\\\ 2x-4y\\end{aligned}],$ find $9\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+4.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)).$\n\nIn order to find $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T)),$ we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has no non-pivot column. \n\nTherefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(T))=0.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n9\\cdot \\text{nullity}(T)+4=9\\cdot 0+4=4.\n$$",
